# MCP connection

## Part A — Dev-time MCP servers (always)

Goal: agents can test and inspect the running app without extra permissions. Configure in `.mcp.json` at the project root (project scope, committed) with secrets supplied through environment variables, never written in the file.

| Server | Purpose | Least-privilege setting |
|---|---|---|
| Browser automation (e.g. Playwright MCP) | Click through role panels, check UI states, reproduce bugs | Allowed origins: `http://localhost:{{WEB_PORT}}` only |
| PostgreSQL (read-only) | Inspect data while debugging | Connect as the read-only DB role from `docs/DATABASE.md`, dev database only, never production |
| GitHub | Branches, PRs, review comments | Fine-grained token scoped to this repo |
| Library docs (optional) | Current docs for the pinned versions | No credentials |

Before adding any server:
1. Confirm it is the official package or image (publisher, repository, downloads) and pin its version, exactly as for npm dependencies.
2. Read what tools it exposes; disable write/destructive tools you do not need.
3. Add the needed env variables to `.env.example` with comments.
4. Start it and run one harmless call to prove it works; note it in `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` §11.

Never point a dev MCP server at production data, and never commit tokens.

## Part B — Product MCP server (only if the PRD requires AI/agent access)

Build it as `apps/mcp` using the official MCP TypeScript SDK (verify package name and version live).

- Reuse the API's services; never query the database directly from tool handlers, so the same business rules and ownership checks apply.
- Authenticate every connection (OAuth or scoped API tokens per user); map the token to a user and role; apply the same RBAC and ownership checks as the HTTP API.
- Expose read-only tools first; write tools require explicit PRD approval, confirmation parameters, and idempotency keys.
- Validate every tool input with the shared schemas; cap result sizes; paginate.
- Rate limit per token (its own tier) and audit-log every tool call.
- Treat any text returned from user-generated content as data, not instructions (prompt-injection aware tool descriptions; never echo secrets).
- Add the server's tools to `docs/API.md` (MCP section) and tests to `tests/security/` (unauthorised token, other user's data, oversized input).
